 AFAIK G4 is a sort of a mix between 32 and 64-bit. 

 As for the "difference" and the "128-bit" part: the G4 ha the so called 
"altivec", whic apple says is a Vector CPU like unit... Actually it's more 
of a MMX-Like unit at PC's, which can do SOME operations on packed data - 
so that you can pack say 8 values into one register, and 8 into the other, 
and than you can do say madd which adds 8 values at a time. 

 Now apple counts thi ONE instruction as 8 ;)) Hence it says that a 1.25GHz 
cpu can 9 9GFLOPS - allthough that means that it can do 1.25G madd or mmul 
or some simmilar oprations per second. 

 Now this DOES come in handy when you do matrixes or something, but it's 
mostly used for stuff like real-time graphics, cause, even with 128-bit 
Altivec register the values can be 16-bit max. (8x16=128 ;) ). Therefor you 
can;'t use this to say solve equasions, or do serious math... 

 For such tasks alpha is, IMHO, still a better choice ;)

 To be fair, altivec enabled games would probably REALY be fast... It does 
well on say photoshop and the likes too... All in all it's great for 
graphics, or any other deskop/workstation use. 

