Jozer <osy@softhome.net> wrote:
> If you are using Windows NT, you are using AlphaBIOS.  Upgrade to the newest
> version, and I know that there are several versions of Linux that run from
> AlphaBIOS, since this is a Linux forum.

_All_ "versions" of Linux, whatever that may mean, run from AlphaBIOS.
This is not a question of "Linux" but of a boot loader.  The one used by
Linux with ARC consoles and their derivatives, like AlphaBIOS, is called
"milo".  If your kernel is 2.4 and above then depending on a milo
version you may need, and sometimes you may not need, a kernel
configured with LEGACY_LOAD_ADDRESS, or something like that (from faulty
memory), set or a machine will not boot.  Making a custom kernel is
not a black magic but finding a better milo version may make your life
easier.  Current distros may have this LEGACY stuff not set in
default kernel because some higher end machines will not boot with it
and it does not matter if you booting via SRM and its 'aboot' loader.
Oh, and keep your boot images, ramdisks, and the like on a small boot
partition of a "very plain" ext2 type (no "big blocks", no "sparse
superblocks", no ext3 ... ).   You will have less headaches.

> I am sure that someone will freak
> out and say that SRM is better.

There are real technical reasons why SRM console is preferable but
there are situations when it cannot be used.  Attempts at dual boot
with NT is one of these.

Keep in mind that ARC firmware has not a clue how to handle BSD type
partitioning and reversely SRM is totally ignorant of FAT.  Because of
that your boot disk needs to have a partion table of an expected type.
Linux kernel will handle many kinds of partition tables, when asked
nicely, so what you use on other, non-boot, devices is up to you.  It is
possible to boot from a floppy, if you have one, or even CD and while
this solves a problem of a "wrong" partitioning it is not much fun for
various reasons.

  Michal


