"Nim" <nim_sathi@nospam.yahoo.com> writes:
> Any ideas how I can access my RAID disks?

Find out what kind of DAC960 you have, there is good information about 
identifying cards at http://www.mylex.com/pub/kbase/dac-ref.txt then go to 
http://www.dandelion.com/Linux/DAC960.html to find out what cards 
requires which firmware version ; check that you have the latest 
DAC960 driver installed (methinks that if you have kernel at least
2.4.12 then you have the latest driver) and go downloading approriate
firmware from http://www.mylex.com/support/productgd/legacypci.html.
You'll need to update the firmware of your DAC960 in order to make it
visible to the driver.  For me the easiest way was to disconnect the
card from alpha box, insert into PCI bus of an intel iron and run the
MS-DOS configuration utility to flash the new firmware. Also I managed
to lose information about my raid sets configured so do make a proper
backup first. 

Or this is the thing that I suspect. If saying
/sbin/modprobe dac960
doesn't help at all. Or upgrading to a more recent kernel and saying
/sbin/modprobe dac960
.. after you have your raid sets visible, you'll need to use fdisk to
create partitions (use BSD disklabel style, not the linux style)
so that the first partition doesn't start from first cylinder but instead
maybe cyl number 4. Then mkfs, mount and use cpio to move your running
system. At least alphaserver 1000 can boot from DAC960 while aboot
is used, I was tricked by not being able to give the kernel my 
root-fs in command line until noticed that it may be given as 
major-minor -number pair like:
0:1/boot/vmlinux-2.4.17-noritake root=3001
(from /etc/aboot.conf) as 
/dev/rd/c0d0p1         1503760   1256628    170744  89% /
$ ls -l /dev/rd/c0d0p1 
brw-------    1 root     root      48,   1 Mar 11  2022 /dev/rd/c0d0p1
where 48 dec = 30 hex. 

But there may be other booting methods too. Don't know. 

