> since the device-name of my os-disk is wrong.
> 
> is it somehow possible to workaroung this behavior ?

If you load your scsi drivers as modules then, because they
are different, an order in which modules are getting inserted
controls in which order disks are detected.  You can force it
the way you like it.  In particular in your initrd do not
load a driver for a your non-boot disk.  It can be loaded later.

Otherwise a detection of PCI devices depends on hardware slots
they are using.

Do you really need two controllers for two disks?  Why?

Or you may equally well boot from /dev/sdb. To fix your current
situation for the first time specify a full boot command with
'root=/dev/sdb<whatever>' and level 1, boot, edit your /etc/fstab and
/etc/aboot.conf and continue like before.  Depending on your startup
scripts you may need to remount your / read-write before trying to edit.
If you were mounting by label then editing /etc/fstab is not needed
beyond adding to it new file systems.

> i want to have the os on the internal-disk, an the
> archives for file-sharing on the external disks.

What stops you?  "Drive letters" are not that important.

  Michal


