Matt Simis <mattsimis@hotmail.com> wrote:
> 
> One thing though, I can boot Linux by simply setting root=/dev/hda2 , no
> need for initrd for me!

If you are trying to use ext3 file system on a root partition then,
unless you recompiled yourself a custom kernel with a required support,
you are actually mounting / as ext2  (see with 'cat /proc/mounts').
In distribution kernels ext3 support, if present, is loaded as a module.
This assumes, of course, that your kernel can "see" a disk with /
without loading anything extra which may, or may not, be the case.

I do not recall now other side-effects of skipping initrd but there can
be some.  Obviously you can always configure and compile yourself
a kernel which has all support you require "built-in" but this is
another story.

  Michal


